.\" AXE modifications: document fixed format order and all-empty headers.
.SS Default format
.PP
Columns: \fIPID\fR \fITTY\fR \fITIME\fR \fICMD\fR.
.TP
.B PID
Process ID, from \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR field 1 (see
\fBproc_pid_stat\fR(5)).
.TP
.B TTY
Controlling terminal name (\fItty2\fR, \fIpts/0\fR, etc.) or
\fB?\fR if the process has none.
.TP
.B TIME
Total CPU time consumed by the process (utime + stime), formatted
as \fIHH:MM:SS\fR.
.TP
.B CMD
Short command name from \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR's \fIcomm\fR field
(see \fBproc_pid_comm\fR(5)). Truncated by the kernel to 16 bytes.
.SS Full format (\-f)
.PP
Columns: \fIUID\fR \fIPID\fR \fIPPID\fR \fIC\fR \fISTIME\fR
\fITTY\fR \fITIME\fR \fICMD\fR. TIME is always \fIHH:MM:SS\fR;
the last column contains the full command line.
.TP
.B UID
Effective user name (or numeric UID if unresolvable).
.TP
.B PPID
Parent process ID.
.TP
.B C
Cumulative CPU usage of the process, truncated to an integer
percentage and capped at 99.
.TP
.B STIME
Start time of the process: \fIHH:MM\fR for same-day starts, a
month-day abbreviation (e.g. \fIApr29\fR) for older starts.
.TP
.B CMD
Full command line from \fI/proc/[pid]/cmdline\fR. Kernel threads,
which have an empty cmdline, are shown bracketed (\fB[kthreadd]\fR).
.SS BSD user format (u, aux)
.PP
Adds \fIUSER\fR, \fI%CPU\fR, \fI%MEM\fR, \fIVSZ\fR, \fIRSS\fR,
\fISTAT\fR, \fISTART\fR.
.TP
.B USER
Same as \fIUID\fR in the full format.
.TP
.B %CPU
Percentage of one CPU's time the process has used since it started
(cumulative, not interval). Truncated to one decimal below 100%;
larger values are printed as whole percentages without a CPU cap.
.TP
.B %MEM
Resident set size as a percentage of total physical memory, truncated
to one decimal and capped at 99.9%.
.TP
.B VSZ
Virtual memory size of the process in kibibytes.
.TP
.B RSS
Resident set size in kibibytes from \fIVmRSS\fR in
\fI/proc/[pid]/status\fR. Zero when the kernel omits that field,
including zombies and kernel threads.
.TP
.B STAT
Multi-character process state. The first character is the kernel
state (\fBR\fR running, \fBS\fR sleeping, \fBD\fR uninterruptible
sleep, \fBT\fR stopped, \fBZ\fR zombie, \fBI\fR idle — see
\fBproc_pid_stat\fR(5)) followed by zero or more modifier flags:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<\fR — high priority (negative nice value)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBN\fR — low priority (positive nice value)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBs\fR — session leader
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl\fR — multi-threaded process
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+\fR — foreground process group on its tty
.RE
.TP
.B START
Same format as \fISTIME\fR in \fB\-f\fR.
.SS Custom format (\-o, \-\-format)
.PP
\fBps \-o COL[,COL...]\fR selects columns by name. Specs may also be
space-separated within a quoted argument or split across multiple
\fB\-o\fR flags; all are concatenated. Append \fB=LABEL\fR to a
column to override its header (\fBpid=ID\fR), or just \fB=\fR to
leave that column's header blank. The header row is suppressed only
when every column has an empty header.
.PP
AIX/SVR4 single-character codes (\fB%p\fR, \fB%U\fR, ...) are also
accepted and resolve to the same fields:
.RS
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
%C@%cpu (alias \fIpcpu\fR)
%G@\fIgroup\fR
%P@\fIppid\fR
%U@\fIuser\fR
%a@\fIargs\fR
%c@\fIcomm\fR
%g@\fIrgroup\fR
%n@\fInice\fR
%p@\fIpid\fR
%r@\fIpgid\fR
%t@\fIetime\fR
%u@\fIruser\fR
%x@\fItime\fR (cputime)
%y@\fItty\fR
%z@\fIvsz\fR
.TE
.RE
.PP
Field names below are listed canonical-first; bracketed forms
(\fB[alias]\fR) accept the same column under a different spelling.
Lookup is case-insensitive.
.SS Identifiers
.TP
.B pid \fR[\fItid\fR, \fIlwp\fR]
Process ID. \fItid\fR/\fIlwp\fR currently alias \fIpid\fR; per-thread
output is deferred.
.TP
.B ppid
Parent process ID.
.TP
.B pgid \fR[\fIpgrp\fR]
Process group ID.
.TP
.B sid \fR[\fIsess\fR]
Session ID.
.TP
.B tpgid
Foreground process group on the controlling terminal.
.SS User and group
.TP
.B uid \fR[\fIeuid\fR]
Effective user ID (numeric).
.TP
.B ruid, suid
Real / saved user ID.
.TP
.B gid \fR[\fIegid\fR], \fBrgid\fR
Effective / real group ID (numeric).
.TP
.B user \fR[\fIuname\fR, \fIeuser\fR], \fBruser\fR
Effective / real user \fIname\fR (resolved via \fI/etc/passwd\fR;
falls back to the numeric UID).
.TP
.B group \fR[\fIegroup\fR], \fBrgroup\fR
Effective / real group name (resolved via \fI/etc/group\fR).
.SS Command
.TP
.B comm \fR[\fIucmd\fR, \fIucomm\fR]
Short command name from \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR (truncated to 16
bytes by the kernel). Header: \fBCOMMAND\fR.
.TP
.B args \fR[\fIcmd\fR, \fIcommand\fR]
Full command line from \fI/proc/[pid]/cmdline\fR; kernel threads
are bracketed. Header: \fBCOMMAND\fR.
.SS State and scheduling
.TP
.B state \fR[\fIs\fR]
Single-letter process state. Header: \fBS\fR.
.TP
.B stat
Multi-character state including modifier letters (see
\fI\-aux\fR section above).
.TP
.B nice \fR[\fIni\fR]
Nice value (\-20..19).
.TP
.B pri
Procps priority: 39 minus the kernel priority from
\fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR. For normal scheduling this is 19 minus
the nice value; larger numbers mean higher priority.
.TP
.B psr \fR[\fIprocessor\fR]
CPU index the process last ran on, or \fB?\fR if not reported.
.TP
.B nlwp \fR[\fIthcount\fR]
Number of threads (kernel \fInum_threads\fR).
.TP
.B cls \fR[\fIclass\fR, \fIpolicy\fR]
Scheduler class abbreviation: \fBTS\fR (SCHED_NORMAL),
\fBFF\fR, \fBRR\fR, \fBB\fR, \fBISO\fR, \fBIDL\fR, \fBDLN\fR.
\fB\-\fR if not reported, \fB?\fR for unrecognised values.
.SS Memory
.TP
.B vsz \fR[\fIvsize\fR]
Virtual memory size in KiB.
.TP
.B rss \fR[\fIrsz\fR]
Resident set size in KiB from \fIVmRSS\fR in
\fI/proc/[pid]/status\fR, not the resident-page count in stat.
Zero when \fIVmRSS\fR is absent.
.TP
.B %mem \fR[\fIpmem\fR]
RSS as a percentage of total physical memory, truncated to one
decimal and capped at 99.9%.
.SS CPU and time
.TP
.B %cpu \fR[\fIpcpu\fR]
Cumulative CPU usage since process start, as a percentage of one
CPU. Truncated to one decimal below 100%, or printed as a whole
percentage at 100% and above. Multi-core usage is not capped.
.TP
.B c
Same as \fI%cpu\fR but truncated to an integer and clamped to 99.
.TP
.B time \fR[\fIcputime\fR]
Total CPU time consumed (utime + stime), \fIHH:MM:SS\fR.
.TP
.B cputimes \fR[\fItimes\fR]
Same as \fItime\fR but printed as integer seconds.
.TP
.B bsdtime
Same as \fItime\fR but printed as \fIMMM:SS\fR (no hour rollover).
.TP
.B etime
Wall-clock elapsed time since process start: \fIMM:SS\fR,
\fIHH:MM:SS\fR, or \fIDD\-HH:MM:SS\fR depending on magnitude.
Whole elapsed seconds are calculated after subtracting the process
start ticks from the boot-clock snapshot ticks.
Header: \fBELAPSED\fR.
.TP
.B etimes
Same as \fIetime\fR but printed as integer seconds.
.TP
.B start \fR[\fIstart_time\fR, \fIstime\fR, \fIbsdstart\fR]
Process start time: \fIHH:MM\fR for same-day starts, a month-day
abbreviation (\fIApr29\fR) otherwise.
.TP
.B lstart
Full timestamp of process start: \fIDay Mon DD HH:MM:SS YYYY\fR
(e.g. \fISat May 3 14:23:45 2026\fR). Header: \fBSTARTED\fR.
.SS Other
.TP
.B tty \fR[\fItt\fR, \fItname\fR]
Controlling terminal name; \fB?\fR for none.
.TP
.B fds
Number of entries in \fI/proc/[pid]/fd\fR. \fB?\fR if the directory
is unreadable for the calling user.
.TP
.B wchan
Symbolic name of the kernel function the process is sleeping in;
\fB\-\fR for runnable or unknown.
.TP
.B flags \fR[\fIf\fR]
The three legacy flag bits from \fI/proc/[pid]/stat\fR, printed as
one octal digit: \fI(flags >> 6) & 7\fR. Other kernel flag bits
do not contribute. Header: \fBF\fR.
.SS Signal masks
.PP
The four signal-mask fields print 16 hexadecimal characters of the
corresponding bitmask from \fI/proc/[pid]/status\fR.
.TP
.B pending \fR[\fIsig\fR, \fIsig_pend\fR]
Pending signals (\fISigPnd\fR).
.TP
.B blocked \fR[\fIsig_block\fR, \fIsigmask\fR]
Blocked signals (\fISigBlk\fR).
.TP
.B ignored \fR[\fIsig_ignore\fR, \fIsigignore\fR]
Ignored signals (\fISigIgn\fR).
.TP
.B caught \fR[\fIsig_catch\fR, \fIsigcatch\fR]
Caught (handler-installed) signals (\fISigCgt\fR).
